  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nEarly this morning GOES water vapor imagery and 500mb model height \nanalysis showed an upper level ridge extending from the southern \nplains north into the Canadian Prairies, resulting in northwest flow \naloft over the Great Lakes. At the surface troughing extended \nthrough much of the high plains, in the lee of the Rockies with high \npressure over Ontario, resulting in light northeasterly flow over \nthe U.P. GOES fog and microphysics products showed widespread low \nstratus and fog over the western and southern halves of the U.P. \nDense fog had developed in some locations where fog was being \nenhanced by upslope flow, such as the higher elevations of Marquette \nCounty. Temperatures were in the upper 50s where skies had cleared \nin the east and far north (Keweenaw) with low to mid 60s elsewhere.\n\nNot much change is expected in the pattern today into Sunday with an \noverall warm and pleasant Labor Day Weekend expected. Surface high \npressure over Ontario will continue to extend a ridge south into the \nU.P. with a weak pressure gradient resulting in light northeasterly \nflow. Locally dense fog can be expected to start out the day over \nthe western and southern halves of the U.P. Did not issue any \nheadlines for the fog as it seems confined to favored upslope areas \nand not widespread. Expect fog and low clouds to diminish through \nmid-morning as diurnal heating mixes out the low levels. \nTemperatures will warm well into the 70s with abundant sunshine this \nafternoon over the north and east though scattered clouds will \nlinger over parts of the western U.P. With clear skies and light \nwinds expect good radiational cooling tonight with temperatures \nfalling into the 40s away from the shorelines. Patchy fog may again \ndevelop tonight (30-50% chance), mainly in the central and eastern \nU.P. For the remainder of the Labor Day weekend expect highs in the \nupper 70s to low 80s along with partly cloudy skies Sunday, giving \nway to increasing cloud cover Monday afternoon as a system \napproaches the area.\n\nPrecip chances return Monday night from the west, continuing \ninto Tuesday night as the upper level ridge moves east and \nflattens. This gives way to the deep western trough to lift \nnortheast to Manitoba by the end of the day Tuesday, then \ntracking east to Quebec. A resulting sfc low from the northern \nRockies lifts to Manitoba by Tuesday morning, then moves across \nfar northern Ontario to Quebec through Wednesday. Showers and \nstorms return to the UP ahead of the associated cold front, \nmainly on Tuesday. This does not look to bring much in the way \nof strong storms, but it does present the best rainfall chances \nas of recent. NBM probabilities of at least 1 inch of rain in 24\nhours are between 30-50%. This system also yields breezy winds.\nInitially expecting south winds on Tuesday ahead of the cold \nfront gusting between 15-30 mph, particulary in ideal southerly \ndownslope flow. Behind the front, pressure rises and CAA support\nstronger westerly winds on Wednesday with frequent gusts of \n20-30 mph. Probabilities for wind gusts exceeding 40 mph are \nbetween 30-60% in the Keweenaw.\n\nThere is a slight chance (15-24% chance) for additional showers late \nWednesday into Thursday with a shortwave to the north and a \nsecondary cold front pressing south. Otherwise temps return near \nnormal in the mid 60s to mid 70s late next week.",
